---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Compare from "../components/Compare.astro";
import Sankey from "../components/Sankey.astro";
import comparisons from "../data/comparisons.json";
import { recommendations, findings, sources, gradeText, url } from "../lib/graph";
const used = sources.filter((s: any) => s.used).length;
const overview = comparisons.find((c) => c.id === "bx24-hills");
const squares = comparisons.filter((c) => c.id !== "bx24-hills");
---
<Base title="Overview" current="">
  <h1>Making New Zealand topo maps easier to read</h1>
  <p class="lede">An open research project re-styling LINZ's topographic map layers for people with colour vision deficiency, dyslexia, low vision and ageing eyes, and for field conditions like photocopies and red headtorches.</p>
  <p>The target is Topo50 as served in LINZ Basemaps' <code>topo-raster-gridded</code> layer. The current work re-renders that map from LINZ's own QGIS project. The map data stays exactly as LINZ publishes it. Only how it's drawn changes, and every change can be traced back to the evidence behind it. Today that's {used} sources, {findings.length} findings and {recommendations.length} recommendations. A window of sheet BX24 is below, about 17 km by 16 km, stock beside the accessible raster. The close views are four 1 km squares.</p>

  <Compare c={overview} />

  <figure class="wide">
    <h2 style="margin-top:1.5rem">From evidence to map</h2>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">Read left to right. Each band is one citation. Hover over or tab to any box to trace its full chain.</p>
    <Sankey />
    <figcaption>
      Bands are shaded by the strength of the evidence they carry:
      <span class="grade a">A</span> {gradeText.a.slice(3)};
      <span class="grade b">B</span> {gradeText.b.slice(3)};
      <span class="grade c">C</span> {gradeText.c.slice(3)};
      <span class="grade d">D</span> {gradeText.d.slice(3)}.
      The same information is in the <a href={url("trace/")}>evidence trail</a> as tables.
      The last column, Style changes, is the web vector style. Current attention is on the topographic raster style.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Raster squares</h2>
  <ul>
    {squares.map((c) => <li><a href={url(`see/#${c.id}`)}>{c.title}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
  <p><a href={url("see/")}>See how the maps look to different people</a>. <a href={url("inks/")}>See the ink contrast pairs</a>.</p>
</Base>
